module

public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.DiscreteValuationRing.EppMixedCharacteristic
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.DiscreteValuationRing.ExtensionLevel

/-!
# The residue condition of Tag 09F5 under a totally ramified base change

Hypothesis (5) of Tag 09F5 is the residue condition `κ_A = ⋂_{n ≥ 1} κ_B^{pⁿ}` on an extension
`ρ : κ_A → κ_B` of residue fields. This file proves that the condition transports along a square
of residue fields whose vertical maps `κ_{A₁} ≃ₐ[A] κ_A` and `κ_{B₁} ≃ₐ[B] κ_B` are algebra
equivalences, provided the square commutes. The square commutes whenever `κ_{A₁}` is generated by
the image of `A`, since both routes around it are then `A`-algebra maps out of `κ_{A₁}` that agree
on that image; this holds when `A₁` is totally ramified over `A`, the residue extension
`κ_A → κ_{A₁}` then being trivial.

The file also bounds the extension level of a degree `p` extension, and characterizes weak
unramifiedness by the equality of maximal ideals.

## The transport, and what makes it work

The condition is a statement about the **range** of `ρ : κ_A → κ_B`, so transporting it needs more
than isomorphisms `κ_{A₁} ≅ κ_A` and `κ_{B₁} ≅ κ_B` separately: it needs them to commute with the
two horizontal maps. `forall_exists_pow_iff_of_algEquiv_square` is the transport given that square,
and `algEquiv_square_comm_of_surjective` is the square — obtained not by computation but from the
**variance** of the two equivalences:

* `eA : κ_{A₁} ≃ₐ[A] κ_A` is an `A`-algebra equivalence;
* `eB : κ_{B₁} ≃ₐ[B] κ_B` is a `B`-algebra one, hence in particular an `A`-algebra one;
* and `κ_{A₁}` is generated by the image of `A`, because the residue extension `κ_A → κ_{A₁}` is
  *trivial* — which is exactly what "totally ramified" means.

So both routes around the square are `A`-algebra maps out of something the image of `A` fills up,
and therefore agree. The third bullet is the only input that is specific to the situation, and it
is what `surjective_residueField_map_of_totallyRamified` below records.

This is why the ascent failure of the equal-characteristic branch does not bite here. There are
finite extensions along which the residue condition genuinely fails to ascend — but such an
extension *grows* the residue field (for instance `𝔽_p(t)` to `𝔽_{p²}(t)`).
`IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsTotallyRamifiedWithRespectTo` is by definition "unique maximal ideal
above, **and** trivial residue field extension", so no growth happens and the obstruction is
absent.

## Where the two verticals come from

* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_residueField_algEquiv_of_totallyRamified` — the downstairs
  vertical `κ_{A₁} ≃ₐ[A] κ_A`;
* `IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_residueField_algEquiv_of_tensorProduct` — the upstairs vertical
  `κ_{C₁} ≃ₐ[B] κ_B`, whose four hypotheses are all supplied by Tag 09ER together with Tag 09F5's
  own hypothesis (2).

## Main results

* `Stacks.DegreePExtensionLevel.extensionLevel_nonneg`: the extension level is nonnegative.
* `Stacks.DegreePExtensionLevel.extensionLevel_eq_zero_iff`: the extension level is zero if and
  only if the infimum of the admissible numerators is zero.
* `Stacks.DegreePExtensionLevel.extensionLevel_lt_of_isDegreePExtensionOfFiniteLevel`: the
  extension level of a degree `p` extension of finite level is less than `p`, from the plain
  finiteness hypothesis.
* `Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.surjective_residueField_map_of_totallyRamified`:
  the residue map of a totally ramified extension is surjective.
* `Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.isWeaklyUnramified_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq`: an
  extension of discrete valuation rings in which `𝔪_A` generates `𝔪_B` is weakly unramified, and
  `isWeaklyUnramified_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq_of_ringHom_eq` for a map only *known to agree* with
  `algebraMap`.
* `surjective_algebraMap_residueField_of_surjective_map` and
  `surjective_algebraMap_residueField_of_totallyRamified`: surjectivity of `A → κ_B`, deduced
  from surjectivity of `κ_A → κ_B`.
* `Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.algEquiv_square_comm_of_surjective`: the square
  of residue fields commutes when `A` surjects onto the upper-left corner, by variance.
* `Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.forall_exists_pow_iff_of_algEquiv_square`: the
  residue condition transports across a commuting square whose verticals are isomorphisms.
* `Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.forall_exists_pow_iff_of_surjective`: the
  residue condition transports across such a square when `A` surjects onto the upper-left corner.

## References

* [The Stacks Project, Tag 09ER](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09ER)
* [The Stacks Project, Tag 09F5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09F5)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

namespace Stacks.DegreePExtensionLevel

/-! ### The range of the extension level -/

/-- The extension level `extensionLevel A K L P π e₁ he₁ hne` is nonnegative.

The level is a quotient of two natural numbers, so it is nonnegative for any `P`, `π` and `e₁`,
with no finiteness hypothesis. The upper bound `level < p` is the half that genuinely needs
finiteness. -/
theorem extensionLevel_nonneg
    (A : Type*) (K : Type*) (L : Type*)
    [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [Field K] [Field L]
    [Algebra A K] [Algebra K L]
    (P : Polynomial A) (π : A) (e₁ : ℕ) (he₁ : 0 < e₁)
    (hne : (extensionLevelNumerators A K L P π).Nonempty) :
    0 ≤ extensionLevel A K L P π e₁ he₁ hne :=
  div_nonneg (Nat.cast_nonneg _) (Nat.cast_nonneg _)

/-- The extension level is zero if and only if the infimum of the admissible numerators
`extensionLevelNumerators A K L P π` is zero.

The level is `sInf (extensionLevelNumerators …) / e₁` with `e₁ > 0`, so it vanishes exactly when
the infimum of the admissible numerators does — that is, when some generator has `ξ` already
integral, with no negative power of `π` needed. -/
theorem extensionLevel_eq_zero_iff
    (A : Type*) (K : Type*) (L : Type*)
    [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [Field K] [Field L]
    [Algebra A K] [Algebra K L]
    (P : Polynomial A) (π : A) (e₁ : ℕ) (he₁ : 0 < e₁)
    (hne : (extensionLevelNumerators A K L P π).Nonempty) :
    extensionLevel A K L P π e₁ he₁ hne = 0 ↔
      sInf (extensionLevelNumerators A K L P π) = 0 := by
  rw [extensionLevel, div_eq_zero_iff]
  simp [he₁.ne']

/-- If `L / K` is a degree `p` extension of finite level and `wElt f` is associated to `π ^ e` for
a uniformizer `π`, then the set of admissible numerators is nonempty and the extension level is
less than `p`.

The finiteness datum gives a generator `z`, a parameter `ξ` and a witness that `w ^ p * ξ` is
integral with `π` dividing it, and
`Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.mul_pow_sub_one_mul_mem_range` converts that into
integrality of `π ^ (p * e - 1) * ξ`. So `p * e - 1` is an admissible numerator, the infimum of the
numerators is at most it, and dividing by `e` puts the level below `p`. The `Nonempty` witness is
returned alongside because `extensionLevel` takes it as an argument. -/
theorem extensionLevel_lt_of_isDegreePExtensionOfFiniteLevel
    (A K L : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    [Field K] [Field L] [Algebra A K] [Algebra K L]
    [IsFractionRing A K] [Module.Finite K L]
    (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (f : CyclotomicRing p ℤ ℚ →+* A) (P : Polynomial A)
    (hfinite : IsDegreePExtensionOfFiniteLevel A K L p f P)
    (π : A) (hπ : Irreducible π) (e : ℕ) (he : 0 < e)
    (hw : Associated (wElt f) (π ^ e)) :
    ∃ hne : (extensionLevelNumerators A K L P π).Nonempty,
      extensionLevel A K L P π e he hne < (p : ℚ) := by
  obtain ⟨-, ξ, z, hz, hPz, a, ha, heq⟩ := hfinite
  have hπL : algebraMap A K π ≠ 0 :=
    (map_ne_zero_iff _ (IsFractionRing.injective A K)).mpr hπ.ne_zero
  rw [hπ.maximalIdeal_eq, Ideal.mem_span_singleton] at ha
  have hpe : 0 < p * e := Nat.mul_pos (Fact.out : p.Prime).pos he
  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction.mul_pow_sub_one_mul_mem_range π
    (wElt f) hπL p e hpe hw ξ a ha heq
  have hcandidate : p * e - 1 ∈ extensionLevelNumerators A K L P π :=
    ⟨ξ, z, hz, hPz, b, hb⟩
  refine ⟨⟨p * e - 1, hcandidate⟩, ?_⟩
  simp only [extensionLevel]
  rw [div_lt_iff₀ (by exact_mod_cast he)]
  exact_mod_cast lt_of_le_of_lt (Nat.sInf_le hcandidate) (Nat.sub_lt hpe (by decide))

end Stacks.DegreePExtensionLevel

namespace Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction

/-! ### A totally ramified extension has trivial residue extension -/

/-- If `E / K` is totally ramified with respect to `A`, then the map from the residue field of `A`
to the residue field of the integral closure of `A` in `E` is surjective.

`IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_residueField_algEquiv_of_totallyRamified` says the residue field of
a totally ramified extension *is* that of the base. This is the same fact in the form the square
argument consumes: the residue extension being trivial says the residue field upstairs is filled by
the image of the one downstairs. The two forms are not interderivable for free — going from the
equivalence to this statement means identifying the equivalence with the structure map at whichever
`Algebra` instance is in force — so this is proved independently. -/
theorem surjective_residueField_map_of_totallyRamified
    (A K E : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    [Field K] [Field E] [Algebra A K] [IsFractionRing A K]
    [Algebra K E] [Algebra A E] [IsScalarTower A K E]
    [FiniteDimensional K E] [Algebra.IsSeparable K E]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing (integralClosure A E)]
    [IsLocalHom (algebraMap A (integralClosure A E))]
    (htot : IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsTotallyRamifiedWithRespectTo A K E) :
    Function.Surjective
      (IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map (algebraMap A (integralClosure A E))) := by
  let S := integralClosure A E
  obtain ⟨m, hm, -, _, hf⟩ := htot
  obtain rfl := IsLocalRing.eq_maximalIdeal hm
  let := Ideal.Quotient.algebraOfLiesOver (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal S)
    (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A)
  let : Field (A ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) := Ideal.Quotient.field _
  have : Module.Free (A ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) (S ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal S) :=
    Module.Free.of_divisionRing _ _
  have hfmax : Module.finrank (A ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A)
      (S ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal S) = 1 := by simpa using hf
  exact (Algebra.finrank_eq_one_iff_bijective_algebraMap.mp hfmax).2

/-! ### Weak unramifiedness -/

/-- An extension `A → B` of discrete valuation rings in which the maximal ideal of `A` generates
the maximal ideal of `B` is weakly unramified.

This is the uniqueness clause of `IsDiscreteValuationRing.ramificationIndex_characterization`: the
ramification index is the *only* exponent `e ≥ 1` with
`Ideal.map (algebraMap A B) (𝔪 A) = 𝔪 B ^ e`, so an equality at `e = 1` pins it to `1`. -/
theorem isWeaklyUnramified_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq
    (A B : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    [CommRing B] [IsDomain B] [IsDiscreteValuationRing B]
    [Algebra A B] [IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsExtension A B]
    (h : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).map (algebraMap A B) = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal B) :
    IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsWeaklyUnramified A B :=
  ((IsDiscreteValuationRing.ramificationIndex_characterization A B).2.2.1 1 le_rfl
    (by simpa using h)).symm

/-- Let `g : A →+* B` agree pointwise with `algebraMap A B`. If the maximal ideal of `A` generates
the maximal ideal of `B` along `g`, then `B` is weakly unramified over `A`.

Tag 09ER states its maximal-ideal equalities for the homomorphisms it builds —
`Algebra.TensorProduct.includeRight.toRingHom` for `B₁`, and that composed with the `B₁ ≃ₐ[B] C₁`
for `C₁` — not for `algebraMap`. This takes the equality for such a map together with the
pointwise identification. -/
theorem isWeaklyUnramified_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq_of_ringHom_eq
    (A B : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    [CommRing B] [IsDomain B] [IsDiscreteValuationRing B]
    [Algebra A B] [IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsExtension A B]
    (g : A →+* B) (hg : ∀ a, g a = algebraMap A B a)
    (h : (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).map g = IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal B) :
    IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsWeaklyUnramified A B :=
  isWeaklyUnramified_of_map_maximalIdeal_eq A B (by rwa [← RingHom.ext hg])

/-! ### Surjectivity onto the residue field -/

/-- If the residue map `κ_A → κ_B` of a local homomorphism `A → B` is surjective, then so is
`algebraMap A κ_B`.

The two are the same statement once composed with `residue A`, which is surjective
(`IsLocalRing.residue_surjective`). The `Algebra A κ_B` and `IsScalarTower A B κ_B` instances used
are the ones Mathlib installs for any `[Algebra A B]`, so nothing is chosen here. -/
theorem surjective_algebraMap_residueField_of_surjective_map
    {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [IsLocalRing A] [IsLocalRing B]
    [Algebra A B] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap A B)]
    (h : Function.Surjective (IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map (algebraMap A B))) :
    Function.Surjective (algebraMap A (IsLocalRing.ResidueField B)) := by
  intro y
  obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := h y
  obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := IsLocalRing.residue_surjective x
  refine ⟨a, ?_⟩
  rw [IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply A B (IsLocalRing.ResidueField B),
    IsLocalRing.ResidueField.algebraMap_eq]
  exact (IsLocalRing.ResidueField.map_residue (algebraMap A B) a).symm.trans hx

/-- If `E / K` is totally ramified with respect to `A`, then `algebraMap` from `A` to the residue
field of the integral closure of `A` in `E` is surjective.

This is `surjective_residueField_map_of_totallyRamified` composed with
`surjective_algebraMap_residueField_of_surjective_map`, and it is precisely the `hsurj` hypothesis
of `algEquiv_square_comm_of_surjective`: with it the residue square of a totally ramified base
change commutes with no further input. -/
theorem surjective_algebraMap_residueField_of_totallyRamified
    (A K E : Type*) [CommRing A] [IsDomain A] [IsDiscreteValuationRing A]
    [Field K] [Field E] [Algebra A K] [IsFractionRing A K]
    [Algebra K E] [Algebra A E] [IsScalarTower A K E]
    [FiniteDimensional K E] [Algebra.IsSeparable K E]
    [IsDiscreteValuationRing (integralClosure A E)]
    [IsLocalHom (algebraMap A (integralClosure A E))]
    (htot : IsDiscreteValuationRing.IsTotallyRamifiedWithRespectTo A K E) :
    Function.Surjective
      (algebraMap A (IsLocalRing.ResidueField (integralClosure A E))) :=
  surjective_algebraMap_residueField_of_surjective_map
    (surjective_residueField_map_of_totallyRamified A K E htot)

/-! ### Commutativity of the residue square -/

/-- Let `ρ : kA →ₐ[A] kB` and `ρ' : kA' →ₐ[A] kB'` be algebra maps, and `eA : kA' ≃ₐ[A] kA` and
`eB : kB' ≃ₐ[B] kB` algebra equivalences. If `algebraMap A kA'` is surjective, then the square

```
kA' --ρ'--> kB'
 | eA         | eB
 v            v
kA  --ρ-->   kB
```

commutes.

Nothing is computed. `eA` is an `A`-algebra map and `eB` a `B`-algebra map, hence also an
`A`-algebra map through the tower; so both `eB ∘ ρ'` and `ρ ∘ eA` are `A`-algebra maps out of
`kA'`, and on `algebraMap A kA' a₀` each is `algebraMap A kB a₀` by `AlgHom.commutes`. Surjectivity
says that is every element. In the intended instance `kA' = κ_{A₁}`, and the surjectivity is
supplied by `surjective_residueField_map_of_totallyRamified` — it is exactly total ramification of
`K₁ / K` that makes the square commute. -/
theorem algEquiv_square_comm_of_surjective
    {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    {kA kB kA' kB' : Type*} [CommRing kA] [CommRing kB] [CommRing kA'] [CommRing kB']
    [Algebra A kA] [Algebra A kA'] [Algebra A kB] [Algebra A kB']
    [Algebra B kB] [Algebra B kB']
    [IsScalarTower A B kB] [IsScalarTower A B kB']
    (ρ : kA →ₐ[A] kB) (ρ' : kA' →ₐ[A] kB')
    (eA : kA' ≃ₐ[A] kA) (eB : kB' ≃ₐ[B] kB)
    (hsurj : Function.Surjective (algebraMap A kA')) :
    ∀ a : kA', eB (ρ' a) = ρ (eA a) := by
  intro a
  obtain ⟨a₀, rfl⟩ := hsurj a
  rw [ρ'.commutes, eA.commutes, ρ.commutes,
    IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply A B kB', eB.commutes,
    ← IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply A B kB]

/-! ### Transport of Epp's residue condition -/

/-- Let `ρ : kA →ₐ[A] kB` and `ρ' : kA' →ₐ[A] kB'` be algebra maps forming a commuting square with
algebra equivalences `eA : kA' ≃ₐ[A] kA` and `eB : kB' ≃ₐ[B] kB`. If the range of `ρ` is the set
`⋂_{n ≥ 1} kB^{pⁿ}` of elements having a `pⁿ`-th root for every `n ≥ 1`, then the range of `ρ'` is
`⋂_{n ≥ 1} kB'^{pⁿ}`.

Being a statement about a *range*, the condition is not transported by the two vertical
isomorphisms separately — the square has to commute, which is `algEquiv_square_comm_of_surjective`.
Given that, the transport is elementwise: `eB` carries `pⁿ`-th roots to `pⁿ`-th roots both ways
because it is a ring map with a ring-map inverse, and it carries the range of `ρ'` to the range of
`ρ` because of the square. -/
theorem forall_exists_pow_iff_of_algEquiv_square
    {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    {kA kB kA' kB' : Type*} [CommRing kA] [CommRing kB] [CommRing kA'] [CommRing kB']
    [Algebra A kA] [Algebra A kA'] [Algebra A kB] [Algebra A kB']
    [Algebra B kB] [Algebra B kB']
    (ρ : kA →ₐ[A] kB) (ρ' : kA' →ₐ[A] kB')
    (eA : kA' ≃ₐ[A] kA) (eB : kB' ≃ₐ[B] kB)
    (hsq : ∀ a : kA', eB (ρ' a) = ρ (eA a)) (p : ℕ)
    (h : ∀ x : kB, (∃ a : kA, ρ a = x) ↔ ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → ∃ y : kB, y ^ p ^ n = x) :
    ∀ x : kB', (∃ a : kA', ρ' a = x) ↔ ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → ∃ y : kB', y ^ p ^ n = x := by
  intro x
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨a, rfl⟩ n hn
    obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := (h (eB (ρ' a))).mp ⟨eA a, (hsq a).symm⟩ n hn
    exact ⟨eB.symm y, by rw [← map_pow, hy, eB.symm_apply_apply]⟩
  · intro hx
    obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := (h (eB x)).mpr fun n hn ↦ by
      obtain ⟨y', hy'⟩ := hx n hn
      exact ⟨eB y', by rw [← map_pow, hy']⟩
    exact ⟨eA.symm a, eB.injective (by rw [hsq, eA.apply_symm_apply, ha])⟩

/-- Let `ρ : kA →ₐ[A] kB` and `ρ' : kA' →ₐ[A] kB'` be algebra maps, and `eA : kA' ≃ₐ[A] kA` and
`eB : kB' ≃ₐ[B] kB` algebra equivalences, with `algebraMap A kA'` surjective. If the range of `ρ`
is `⋂_{n ≥ 1} kB^{pⁿ}`, then the range of `ρ'` is `⋂_{n ≥ 1} kB'^{pⁿ}`.

The only situation-specific input is that `kA'` is filled by the image of `A` — for residue fields
this is triviality of the residue extension, i.e. total ramification. Everything else is variance:
no finiteness, no completeness, and no hypothesis on the upper residue extension beyond its being
an isomorphism over `B`. -/
theorem forall_exists_pow_iff_of_surjective
    {A B : Type*} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B]
    {kA kB kA' kB' : Type*} [CommRing kA] [CommRing kB] [CommRing kA'] [CommRing kB']
    [Algebra A kA] [Algebra A kA'] [Algebra A kB] [Algebra A kB']
    [Algebra B kB] [Algebra B kB']
    [IsScalarTower A B kB] [IsScalarTower A B kB']
    (ρ : kA →ₐ[A] kB) (ρ' : kA' →ₐ[A] kB')
    (eA : kA' ≃ₐ[A] kA) (eB : kB' ≃ₐ[B] kB)
    (hsurj : Function.Surjective (algebraMap A kA')) (p : ℕ)
    (h : ∀ x : kB, (∃ a : kA, ρ a = x) ↔ ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → ∃ y : kB, y ^ p ^ n = x) :
    ∀ x : kB', (∃ a : kA', ρ' a = x) ↔ ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n → ∃ y : kB', y ^ p ^ n = x :=
  forall_exists_pow_iff_of_algEquiv_square ρ ρ' eA eB
    (algEquiv_square_comm_of_surjective ρ ρ' eA eB hsurj) p h

end Stacks.DiscreteValuationRing.EppLevelInduction

end
